# thread_stack_new.tkr — the §10 (plano-s10-multithread-nativo §4.1) thread-stack allocator fixture
# (C backend). It proves the leaf `thread_stack_new` — a spawned thread's stack mapped in Teko over
# the raw `mmap` syscall, with a PROT_NONE guard page laid at the LOW end (stacks grow down toward
# it) via `mprotect`. The fixture maps a stack, checks its TOP (the highest usable address, the
# `stack_top` a future M3 `thread_clone` hands the kernel) is writable, checks the usable byte just
# above the guard page is writable, then `munmap`s the whole region. A failed mapping or an
# unwritable stack -> a nonzero exit; a full round-trip -> exit 0.
#
# Single-threaded: this is the stack-allocation leaf M3 consumes when it flips spawn from pthread to
# raw `clone`; it is validated here ahead of that flip.

Feature: thread_stack_new — a guarded thread stack maps, is writable end to end, and frees cleanly

  Scenario: map_write_top_and_bottom_then_unmap — the stack allocator round-trips a usable stack
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
